# Full Pyparsing incremental identity qualification v13

This is a prospective development qualification under the unchanged explicit
`runtime_state_identity_v5` native source profile. It appends one authored Python
comment to `pyparsing/helpers.py` in a separate copy of the complete public source.
The original public tree, Requests enrollment profiles, and v12 results remain
unchanged. It is not a naturally occurring issue or a repair-quality evaluation.

The frozen driver launches independent baseline and changed-cold processes in
parallel. If both finish successfully, it binds their retained artifacts and starts
the changed-incremental process. That process reconstructs the baseline repository
state through `RepositoryState.from_dict`, reconstructs its root, loads every stored
block, and invokes the native bundle constructor and complete verifier. It then
passes the actual restored state and bundle to the native incremental scan/build
interfaces. No guard is dropped for restored artifacts.

Every worker has the same prospective 900-second bound. This larger qualification
bound includes full-state restoration, repeated block verification, and durable
transport; it does not change the 600-second bound of the context service measured
in v12. A deadline or error retains its process receipt and logs and leaves the
qualification incomplete. Incremental work is not launched if an initial worker
fails. No provider is invoked and no source omission is authorized.

All workers record stage wall/CPU times and an enclosing subprocess wall time.
The enclosing process receipt includes initialization, import, runtime/source
guards, native work, persistence, result hashing and exit. The driver wall interval
starts after its one-time source-copy enrollment and freeze creation, which are
not separately timed in this version. Baseline and changed-cold overlap on a
shared, nonexclusive host. Consequently these numbers must not be reported as
a replicated speedup or complete deployment cost estimate.

The primary comparison checks exact serialized state and root bytes and reads all
cold/incremental SQLite block rows in sorted CID order, comparing actual BLOB bytes.
It covers the complete finite native bundle, including all reachable blocks.
Source controls require fresh materialization to succeed, stale expected state to
reject, old bytes under the changed state to reject, changed bytes under the old
state to reject, and a change between the two native reads to reject.

The independent auditor rehashes every frozen runtime file, checks terminal process
receipts and source inventories, validates parent artifact bindings and source
control outcomes, and rereads every cold/incremental block byte. A successful
result would qualify this one full-repository mutation; it would not prove a
general correctness theorem, token advantage, execution reuse or quality gain.
